data: {"event": "retrieve", "tiers": ["OKF"], "okf_node_paths": ["autonomous_systems.md", "multi_agent_consensus.md"], "rag_scores": [], "sources": [{"source": "ARIA seed note (draft)", "source_type": "okf_graph", "tier": "OKF", "similarity": null, "is_retracted": false, "rank": null, "published": null, "cited": false}, {"source": "Linked via Taxonomy of Autonomous Systems and Operational Boundaries", "source_type": "okf_graph", "tier": "OKF", "similarity": null, "is_retracted": false, "rank": null, "published": null, "cited": false}]}

data: {"event": "citation", "id": 0, "title": "Taxonomy of Autonomous Systems and Operational Boundaries", "path": "autonomous_systems.md", "url": null, "authors": null, "year": 2026, "venue": "ARIA seed note (draft)", "snippet": "An autonomous system is a computational or physical entity capable of perceiving its environment, formulating goal-directed plans, and executing actions over extended operational horizons without continuous human intervention.\n\nTaxonomy of Autonomy Levels (adapted from SAE J3016 and NIST ALFUS):\n- Level 0 (No Automation): System provides warnings or momentary intervention; human controls all execution.\n- Level 1 (Driver / Operator Assistance): Execution of either steering or acceleration/deceleration using environmental information.\n- Level 2 (Partial Automation): Simultaneous execution of multiple sub-tasks with human monitoring and immediate fallback readiness.\n- Level 3 (Conditional Automation): System handles all aspects of dynamic performance within an Operational Design Domain (ODD); human intervenes upon request.\n- Level 4 (High Automation): System sustains complete dynamic performance and automated fallback within defined ODD even if human fails to intervene.\n- Level 5 (Full Automation): Unconditional automated performance across all environmental and operational conditions manageable by human operators.\n\nArchitectural Models:\n1. Sense-Plan-Act (SPA): Classical hierarchical planning through centralized state representations.\n2. Subsumption Architecture: Layered reactive behaviors without centralized world models (Brooks, 1986).\n3. Hybrid Cognitive Architectures: Coupling fast reflex loops with asynchronous symbolic deliberation.\n\nReferences:\n1. SAE International (2021). \"Taxonomy and Definitions for Terms Related to Driving Automation Systems for On-Road Motor Vehicles\", Standard J3016_202104. [verified]\n2. Brooks, R. (1986). \"A robust layered control system for a mobile robot\", IEEE Journal on Robotics and Automation, 2(1), 14-23. [verified]\n3. Huang, H. M., et al. (2005). \"Autonomy Levels for Unmanned Systems (ALFUS) Framework Volume I: Terminology\", NIST Special Publication 1011. [verified]", "source_type": "okf_graph", "tier": "OKF", "doi": null, "arxiv_id": null, "is_retracted": false, "review_status": "draft", "cited": false, "rank": null, "published": null}

data: {"event": "citation", "id": 1, "title": "Byzantine Fault Tolerant Consensus in Multi-Agent Reasoning", "path": "multi_agent_consensus.md", "url": null, "authors": null, "year": 2026, "venue": "ARIA seed note (draft)", "snippet": "Consensus in decentralized multi-agent systems requires establishing agreement on shared state variables despite message delays, network partitions, and adversarial or arbitrary (Byzantine) node failures.\n\nFoundational Protocols and Bounds:\n1. Byzantine Generals Problem: In synchronous systems with unauthenticated messages, consensus is impossible unless more than two-thirds of nodes are non-faulty: $n \\ge 3f + 1$, where $f$ denotes the maximum number of Byzantine failures (Lamport, Shostak, & Pease, 1982).\n2. FLP Impossibility Result: Deterministic asynchronous consensus cannot guarantee liveness in the presence of even a single unannounced crash failure (Fischer, Lynch, & Paterson, 1985).\n3. Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance (PBFT): Provides safety and liveness under partial synchrony using three-phase message exchanges (Pre-prepare, Prepare, Commit) with polynomial complexity $O(n^2)$ (Castro & Liskov, 1999).\n\nApplication to Multi-Agent Reasoning Pipelines:\n- Hypotheses proposed by individual agents require quorum verification ($2f + 1$ matching endorsements) prior to commitment into verified shared memory.\n- In conflicting evidence regimes, agents isolate irreconcilable assertions into auditable dispute logs rather than averaging probabilities into an invalid compromise.\n\nReferences:\n1. Lamport, L., Shostak, R., & Pease, M. (1982). \"The Byzantine Generals Problem\", ACM Transactions on Programming Languages and Systems, 4(3), 382-401. [verified]\n2. Fischer, M. J., Lynch, N. A., & Paterson, M. S. (1985). \"Impossibility of Distributed Consensus with One Faulty Process\", Journal of the ACM, 32(2), 374-382. [verified]\n3. Castro, M., & Liskov, B. (1999). \"Practical Byzantine Fault Tolerance\", In Proceedings of the Third USENIX Symposium on Operating Systems Design and Implementation (OSDI '99), pp. 173-186; extended in ACM Transactions on Computer Systems (TOCS), 20(4), 398-461 (2002). [verified]", "source_type": "okf_graph", "tier": "OKF", "doi": null, "arxiv_id": null, "is_retracted": false, "review_status": "draft", "cited": false, "rank": null, "published": null}

